--TEST--
`Core\Request::route()` answers `?Core\Router\Match`, so a program cannot read the match without saying what it does when nothing matched — `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A boundary, on the absence § 1 states: nothing in the table claimed this
// method and path, which is a served request like any other because matching
// dispatches nothing. So the answer is `?Core\Router\Match` and never a match
// with an empty name, and the case a handler most wants to forget is the one
// the checker makes it write down.
//
// No runtime assertion can show it: a program with no request at all refuses
// before any of this runs, and a served one that matched would answer an
// object either way. The refusal is the pin, and it is the binding that fails.
Core\Router\Match $matched = Core\Request::route();
echo $matched->name() ?? "none", "\n";
var $captures = $matched->params();
echo $matched->param("id") ?? "none", "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Router\Match`, found `null|Core\Router\Match`
   --> case.nvs:11:30
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
